+# Jordan's Four-Week GED Math Study Plan
+
+## Sourced facts
+
+- Jordan scored 80% on number operations, 40% on algebraic reasoning, and 60% on data analysis on the latest 30-item diagnostic; the packet gives no geometry result. [S1]
+- Six of Jordan's nine algebra errors involved signed-number operations or one-step equations. [S1]
+- Algebra Cards A supplies worked examples, matching practice, and an answer key for signed numbers, one-step equations, and two-step equations without requiring printing. [S2]
+- Data Lab B supplies separate 12-question sets on tables and graphs, mean and median, and simple probability, but it does not teach algebra. [S3]
+- Each Mixed Check C form contains 20 questions across algebra, data analysis, and number operations; no validated score conversion or GED-prediction claim is provided. [S4]
+- Jordan wants to be ready for a GED Math practice test in six weeks, prefers one worked example before independent practice, has a basic calculator plus pencil and paper, lacks reliable printer access, can study independently, and has no tutor between sessions. [U1]
+
+## Recommendations
+
+- Put algebra first because it is Jordan's lowest reported diagnostic area, while revisiting data analysis and number operations often enough to check retention.
+- Begin each new skill with one worked example, then hide the solution and have Jordan explain the same steps aloud before starting independent questions.
+- Keep a paper error log with four columns: problem number, my answer, error type, and the corrected rule or step. Review that log at the start of the next session.
+- Use accuracy plus a brief explanation as the measure of mastery. A correct answer with an unexplained guess should be reviewed, not counted as secure.
+- Work on screen and record answers on paper so that the plan does not depend on printer access. Use the calculator only after setting up the operation or equation.
+- Stop each timed set when time expires, mark the stopping point, and score only attempted items. This preserves an honest record while keeping each session within its stated length.
+
+## Uncertainties
+
+- Unknown: Jordan's geometry performance is not reported, so this four-week plan cannot determine whether geometry needs a later intensive review.
+- Unknown: neither the diagnostic nor Mixed Check C supplies a validated GED readiness score, so the plan uses improvement on packet items rather than claiming test readiness.
+- Unknown: no accessibility accommodation, language-support need, or testing accommodation has been reported; the schedule may need revision if Jordan identifies one.
+- Unknown: the six-week practice-test date is not specified, so this plan ends with a result-based next step rather than assigning calendar dates after Week 4.
+
+## Week 1
+
+**Measurable objective:** By the last session, solve at least 8 of 10 selected signed-number and one-step-equation problems correctly and explain the inverse operation used on each equation.
+
+| Day | Duration | Activities |
+| --- | ---: | --- |
+| Monday | 60 minutes | 5 min write the goal; 10 min study one worked signed-number example from Algebra Cards A; 25 min solve Cards 1-6 matching problems; 10 min check answers and label errors; 10 min redo every missed item without looking at the key. |
+| Thursday | 60 minutes | 5 min recall two signed-number rules; 10 min study one worked one-step-equation example from Algebra Cards A; 25 min solve Cards 7-9 practice; 10 min check and log errors; 10 min explain and redo two missed or hardest items. |
+| Saturday | 90 minutes | 10 min review the error log; 15 min study one contrasting worked example; 35 min complete a 10-item signed-number and equation set; 15 min score and classify errors; 15 min correct each error and write one rule to carry forward. |
+
+**Practice set:** Algebra Cards A, Cards 1-9 matching problems, ending with a 10-item set containing four signed-number and six one-step-equation items.
+
+**Progress check:** Score the Saturday 10-item set and record both total accuracy and equation explanations; the target is at least 8 correct with an inverse-operation explanation on all six equations.
+
+**Adjustment rule:** If the score is below 8 of 10 or two explanations are missing, use Week 2 Monday's scheduled 10-minute carry-forward block to redo four corresponding Cards 1-9 items; otherwise, use that block to review the two hardest correct items.
+
+## Week 2
+
+**Measurable objective:** By the last session, solve at least 7 of 8 two-step equations correctly, showing a valid intermediate line and checking each solution by substitution.
+
+| Day | Duration | Activities |
+| --- | ---: | --- |
+| Monday | 60 minutes | 10 min complete the Week 1 carry-forward review; 10 min study one worked two-step equation from Algebra Cards A; 25 min solve Cards 13-15 practice; 10 min check by substitution and log errors; 5 min state the order of inverse operations. |
+| Thursday | 60 minutes | 5 min retrieve the inverse-operation sequence; 10 min study one example with a negative value; 30 min solve Cards 16-18 practice and show intermediate lines; 10 min use the key and substitution to check; 5 min choose two items for later review. |
+| Saturday | 90 minutes | 10 min review selected errors; 15 min rework one example without viewing its solution; 35 min complete an 8-item two-step-equation check; 15 min score steps and answers; 15 min correct missed items and verify all eight answers by substitution. |
+
+**Practice set:** Algebra Cards A, Cards 13-18 matching problems, followed by an eight-item check drawn evenly from those six card topics.
+
+**Progress check:** On the Saturday check, record correct final answers, valid intermediate lines, and substitution checks; meet the objective with at least 7 correct and all eight checks shown.
+
+**Adjustment rule:** If fewer than 7 answers are correct or more than one intermediate line is invalid, use Week 3 Monday's scheduled first 10 minutes to redo two matched examples before beginning data work; otherwise, use that block to review the hardest correct equation.
+
+## Week 3
+
+**Measurable objective:** By the last session, answer at least 9 of 12 tables-and-graphs questions and at least 9 of 12 mean-and-median questions correctly, with the relevant values or computation marked.
+
+| Day | Duration | Activities |
+| --- | ---: | --- |
+| Monday | 60 minutes | 10 min finish any triggered algebra review; 10 min inspect one worked-style table example by tracing labels and units; 25 min answer the Data Lab B tables-and-graphs set; 10 min check with the key and error labels; 5 min rewrite one misread label or scale. |
+| Thursday | 60 minutes | 5 min recall the graph-reading checklist; 10 min work one self-made mean example; 25 min answer the Data Lab B mean-and-median set; 10 min check and classify errors; 10 min recompute every miss and note whether arithmetic or concept caused it. |
+| Saturday | 90 minutes | 10 min review both data error lists; 20 min redo four hardest questions without the key; 35 min complete a 12-item retest made of six table-or-graph and six mean-or-median items; 15 min score it; 10 min explain three answers using units or computations. |
+
+**Practice set:** Data Lab B tables-and-graphs and mean-and-median sets, with a 12-item balanced retest assembled from previously attempted items.
+
+**Progress check:** Record the first-pass score for each 12-question set and the balanced retest score; the target is at least 9 correct in each topic set and 10 of 12 on the retest.
+
+**Adjustment rule:** If either topic set is below 9 of 12, devote the first 15 minutes of Week 4's Monday session to that topic's error labels and two corrected examples before mixed practice.
+
+## Week 4
+
+**Measurable objective:** By the final session, improve from Mixed Check C Form 1 to Form 2 and score at least 16 of 20 on Form 2, including at least 8 of 10 algebra items.
+
+| Day | Duration | Activities |
+| --- | ---: | --- |
+| Monday | 60 minutes | 15 min complete the triggered Data Lab B review or, if none was triggered, review the error log; 25 min complete Mixed Check C Form 1 without the key; 10 min score by domain; 10 min select the three highest-value errors and write the card, set, or rule that addresses each one. |
+| Thursday | 60 minutes | 10 min study one worked example matching the largest Form 1 error group; 30 min redo six targeted items using Algebra Cards A or Data Lab B; 10 min check answers; 10 min complete and score four fresh matching items without help. |
+| Saturday | 90 minutes | 10 min review the error log and testing routine; 40 min complete Mixed Check C Form 2 independently; 15 min score total and domains; 15 min correct all missed items using packet resources; 10 min compare forms and write the next-step decision. |
+
+**Practice set:** Mixed Check C Forms 1 and 2, with the Thursday six-item targeted review selected from Algebra Cards A or Data Lab B according to Form 1 errors.
+
+**Progress check:** Compare Form 1 and Form 2 total and domain counts; the stated target is improvement plus at least 16 of 20 overall and at least 8 of 10 algebra items on Form 2.
+
+**Adjustment rule:** If Form 1 already meets both thresholds, still complete targeted review from its errors; if Form 2 misses either threshold, use the weakest Form 2 domain to choose the next two study sessions.
+
+## Milestones and next step
+
+| Milestone | Evidence to record |
+| --- | --- |
+| Week 1 | Saturday accuracy out of 10, six equation explanations checked, and recurring error types. |
+| Week 2 | Saturday accuracy out of 8, intermediate-line count, and eight substitution checks. |
+| Week 3 | Both first-pass scores out of 12, balanced retest score, and error category by topic. |
+| Week 4 | Form 1 and Form 2 totals, algebra subscores, direction of change, and corrected-error list. |
+
+**Next-step rule:** If Form 2 reaches at least 16 of 20 and at least 8 of 10 in algebra while improving on Form 1, use the remaining time before the practice test for one mixed review and geometry assessment; otherwise, schedule two focused sessions on the lowest Form 2 domain and then repeat a comparable mixed check without claiming that the packet score predicts GED performance.
